#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[Last] of all, the duties of masters are set out by the apostle, and that in this verse, where he notes:

+ The #emph[kinds] of their duties.
+ A #emph[reason] to enforce them.

The #emph[kinds] are set out:

- Generally #emph[(do the same things.)]
- Particularly #emph[(forbearing threatening.)]

The #emph[reason] is taken from the subjection in which masters stand to a higher master #emph[(knowing that your#super[+] Master also.)] And it is amplified:

+ By the equal subjection of masters and servants to that master #emph[(your#super[+] … also.)]
+ By the place where that master is #emph[(in heaven.)]
+ By his attribute, in this phrase #emph[(there is no respect of persons with Him.)]

Because the separate duties of masters are only pointed at here, and wrapped up in general terms, I will (as has been done in the former treatises) set out a distinct order, by which we may better find out their duties, and handle them one after another.

All of them may be brought under these two heads:

+ Care in choosing good servants.
+ Conscience in using them well.

To use their servants well, masters must have an eye to their place and authority, and with respect to it they must:

+ Wisely maintain their authority.
+ Rightly manage it.

The right managing of their authority is noted #emph[generally] in this phrase, #emph[do the same things,] but more #emph[particularly] in #footnote[Col 4:1.] another place it is referred to these two branches:

+ Justice.
+ Equity.

Of these in order.

=== §. 2. #emph[Of masters choosing good servants.]

The first thing that a man who takes it on himself to be a master must take care of is to receive good servants into his service. “My eyes#footnote[Ps 101:6.] shall be on the faithful of the land,” says David, “that they may dwell with me; he who walks in the way of integrity shall serve me.” What does this phrase #emph[(my eyes shall be on the faithful)] imply, but that he will diligently and carefully inquire after such men? Indeed, #emph[my eyes] (he says), not another man’s eyes: he would not put all the trust on others; he would himself make what test and trial he could. The phrase #emph[(my eyes)] has the same emphasis where Job#footnote[Job 19:27.] says, “I will see Him for myself; my eyes will behold Him, and not another.”

1\. Servants are, of all other things (except wife and children), #footnote[Reasons.] of the best and greatest use. If, then, men are careful in choosing other things well, such as houses, land, household goods, horses and all kinds of livestock (as men are very circumspect in these), should they not be much more careful in choosing servants well?

2\. In this way masters will show that they seek and aim at the good of their family, and indeed, in their family, at the good of church and commonwealth; for good members of a family are the likeliest to turn out profitable to church and commonwealth.

3\. When good servants are chosen, there is hope of receiving more good from them, and of doing more good to them. They will be pliable to all good admonition, teachable by all good instruction, serviceable in all things they take in hand.

#emph[Objection.] A master may make a bad servant into a good one.

#emph[Answer.] There is great risk and hazard in that, especially if they have grown to ripe years. No creature is easily tamed, or brought from the natural course it has taken, after it has grown up; a bough that has grown crooked for many years on end will hardly be made straight. It is not in man’s power to make a bad servant good; nor can a man expect God’s blessing on it when he does not follow a right course warranted by God.

#emph[Question.] What course is fittest for the choice of servants? #footnote[Direction for choosing servants.]

1\. If they are young, see how they have been brought up from their infancy. The benefit of a good upbringing is great, and much good may be expected from it, for it is the means which God has sanctified for good.

But if they have grown to ripe years, choose such as fear God; such were those on whom David#footnote[Ps 101:6.] cast his eyes.

On this ground #footnote[#emph[Philem. v.] 10.] Saint Paul advised Philemon to take Onesimus back, because grace had then been worked in his heart.

1\. The fear of God is the ground of all good obedience and #footnote[Why servants who fear God are to be chosen.] faithfulness. Note the examples of the good and faithful servants who are commended in Scripture, and you#super[+] will find that they all feared God.

2\. Such servants will not only be diligent in their work, but will also faithfully call on God to prosper the things which they take in hand on their master’s behalf, as the example of Abraham’s#footnote[Gen 24:12.] servant shows.

3\. God will have such regard for servants who fear his name as to bless their master’s house for their sakes. So Potiphar’s#footnote[Gen 39:5.] house was blessed for Joseph’s sake.

2\. Make some trial of their fitness for the calling, work and service to which you will put them. From such servants there may be hope of profit and benefit by their service. Laban#footnote[Gen 29:14.] made trial of Jacob for a month before he made any agreement with him for a long continuance.

3\. Take especially those who are of lowly and poor condition, and do not know how to support themselves except by service. In this way a double work of charity will be done, and in this way you may look for better service; for commonly such servants are the most industrious and the most obedient to their masters.

#emph[Objection.] When men have taken all the care they can in their choice, they may still be deceived.

#emph[Answer.] 1. Then it is much more likely that they will be deceived if they are careless in it.

2\. Diligent and wise inquiry is a means of finding out the disposition and ability of servants.

3\. If men are deceived after using the means, they may have the more comfort in bearing the cross, because they have not knowingly or carelessly pulled it on themselves.

=== §. 3. #emph[Of masters’ carelessness in choosing servants.]

Contrary to this is the carelessness of those who are ready to receive anyone into their house, even atheists, papists, swearers, swaggerers, profane wretches, unclean persons and the like, against whom David protests: “No one who practices deceit shall dwell in #footnote[Ps 101:7.] my house; no one who tells lies shall stand in my presence.” Those who take in such people make their houses cages of unclean birds, seedbeds of wicked persons and unprofitable members; for one scabbed sheep is enough to infect a whole flock. It is no wonder if many injuries fall on such a family, for a man might as well bring so many snakes and adders into his house as such persons; indeed, with them God’s curse is brought in. Can any good be looked for, then? Such foolish masters regard neither themselves nor their household, no, nor church or commonwealth either. If no one would take such people in, it might be a means to make them change their ways.

Many are not only careless in choosing good servants, but even object to those who are religious, thinking it a matter of reproach to take them in. By this notion they often drive God’s blessing away from their house.

Others insist so much on a large sum of money with an apprentice, a clerk or another servant that they will take none but rich men’s children. So it comes about that this point of charity (which is a great point) is neglected: persons most unfit for service are taken in, and many times such as scorn to do service. So their masters grow weary of them; the servants lose their time and never become masters of their craft; and their parents regret giving such a sum with them.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of masters maintaining their authority.]

After masters have chosen good servants, their duty is to use them well; and because of the difference between masters and servants this cannot be done well unless masters wisely maintain their authority. A master therefore must be able “to rule his own household well.”#footnote[1 Tim 3:4 τοῦ ἰδίου οἴκου καλῶς προϊστάμενον.] This is a duty which the apostle requires of a bishop in particular, who is master of a house, but it belongs in general to all masters of families. Women also, who are mistresses by virtue of their places, are commanded #footnote[1 Tim 5:14 οἰκοδεσποτεῖν.] “to govern their households,” or to rule, and to play the part of a mistress in them. It was the #footnote[Matt 8:9.] centurion’s commendation that, having servants under him, he had them at his command.

1\. In this way the image and authority of God, which a master carries, is preserved.

2\. In this way a master will have much better service done. Not one servant in a thousand who is not kept under authority will do good service. A duty like this was enjoined on a husband in relation to his wife, Treatise 4, §. 4. Some of the reasons, directions and other points handled there may be applied here. Read it, therefore.

To this end three things must be observed. #footnote[Direction.]

1\. That masters conduct themselves worthily of their place, and of the honor which is due to them. This may best be done by making themselves a pattern of such good things as belong to them in their places. “As for me and my house, we will serve the #smallcaps[Lord],”#footnote[Josh 24:15.] says Joshua: he would not only set them to it, but would also do it himself; he would go before them. “I will behave wisely … I will walk in my house with integrity of heart,” says #footnote[Ps 101:2.] David.

2\. That masters keep their servants in awe and fear. #footnote[1 Tim 3:4.] Children must be “kept in subjection”; much more servants.

3\. That masters do what they do in their conduct toward their servants with authority. Command, forbid, rebuke (to use #footnote[Titus 2:15.] the apostle’s phrase) “with all authority.” The manner of speech which the centurion used to his servants#footnote[Matt 8:9.] (“Go,” “Come,” “Do this”) carries the ring of authority. So the church, taking on herself the person of a mistress, uses a word of authority: “I charge you#super[+]”#footnote[Song 3:5.] not to do this.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of masters causing their authority to be despised.]

The failings in the defect, contrary to the duty #footnote[How masters lose their honor.] and point of wisdom named above, are many, such as these:

1\. When masters conduct themselves in a base and abject way before #footnote[1\. By base conduct.] their servants: being frivolous in their behavior, foolish in their conduct, given to drunkenness, uncleanness, wicked company and other vices. Nabal was such a man, for his servant could say of him, “He is such a scoundrel.” David,#footnote[1 Sam 25:17.] though he did not give himself over to such foul sins as these, yet often failed by unseemly conduct before his servants: as when “he changed his behavior and feigned madness … he scratched on the doors of the gate and let his saliva run down his beard”;#footnote[1 Sam 21:13.] and again, #footnote[2 Sam 19:3, 5.] when he gave such free rein to his grief for the death of his traitorous son Absalom that his people, being ashamed, stole away from him, and Joab his servant was forced to tell him of it bluntly, saying, “Today you have disgraced all your servants,” etc.

Mistresses often lose their authority by conspiring with their servants to go out, take away goods, gossip, and do other such things secretly without their husbands’ consent. In this way they make themselves slaves to their servants, not daring to do anything which may offend them, for fear that the servants should reveal to their masters such wicked pranks as their mistresses have played.

2\. When masters are too lax and timid, begging and #footnote[2\. By laxness.] pleading with their servants to do such things as they ought to command and require of them; and if it is not done, their only remedy is patience, or else to do it themselves. However much this might be counted meekness and gentleness toward equals and strangers, over whom we have no authority, yet toward servants it is too base a laxness; indeed, it is a giving up of the power which God has given, and of which God will take an account.

3\. When masters allow their servants to be their companions, #footnote[3\. By familiarity.] playing, drinking and reveling with them, and saying (as the proverb has it) #emph[hail fellow, well met.] In this way servants often take the liberty to set themselves above their master; for men are naturally prone to ambition, and #emph[if an inch is given, they will take a mile.] Those who debase themselves so far in this way as to give their servants power over their own body make both themselves and their true, lawful spouse despised: #emph[themselves,] because servants who have been made one flesh with them in this way will expect to keep in awe the master or mistress they have known, who will fear their revealing that sin; their #emph[spouse,] because such servants will expect to be maintained and bolstered up by the master or mistress whom they have known in this way. On this ground Sarah#footnote[Gen 16:3, 4.] was despised in the eyes of Hagar her maid.

4\. When masters are overruled by their servants into doing any #footnote[4\. By letting go the reins.] unjust or unlawful thing: as Joash,#footnote[2 Chr 24:17.] who was drawn into idolatry by his servants; and Zedekiah,#footnote[Jer 38:5.] who gave the prophet Jeremiah into his servants’ hands, using this base and abject speech: “The king can do nothing to stop you#super[+].”#footnote[Jer 38:5.] In this way servants will soon become masters; and if they once come to this high pitch, to rule rather than to be ruled, they will quickly become intolerable. For #footnote[Prov 30:22.] this is one of the four things, noted by the wise man, under which “the earth … cannot bear up”: namely, “a servant who becomes king.” And #footnote[Eccl 10:5, 7.] this is one of the evils which proceed from rulers, “servants on horseback,” that is, servants advanced above their place and rank; from which it follows that “princes go on foot like servants”:#footnote[Eccl 10:5, 7.] they are cast down below their place and rank. It happens in this case between servants and masters as between scales or balances: if the weights that usually lie in one pan to keep it down are taken away, it will suddenly fly up, and so the other pan will be kept down.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of masters’ too great severity.]

The contrary in the excess is too great severity and harshness, shown in look, speech and actions.

1\. In #emph[look,] when a master cannot look kindly on his servant. Jacob#footnote[Gen 31:2, 5.] was much disheartened by the countenance of his master. Many masters greatly terrify their servants by their continually frowning brows and fiery eyes.

2\. In #emph[speech,] when masters cannot give their servants a good word, but if they are moved ever so little, heap on them all the reproachful names they can call to mind, little thinking of this fearful sentence: “anyone who says, ‘You fool!’ will be subject to the fire of hell.”#footnote[Matt 5:22.]

In this way Saul showed a malicious and spiteful mind against Jonathan and David by the foul language he used. Some have such a perverse and crooked tongue that they can never speak plainly to their servants: if they command or forbid a thing, they will do it in such a way that their servant can hardly tell what they mean; and this not only when their servant has offended them, but in their best mood. Others are so shrewish that their tongues are seldom still: they are always scolding on every small occasion. So it comes about that their servants are no more moved by it than the doves and starlings that live continually in belfries are moved by the ringing of the bells. Such bitterness is also mixed with the scoldings of many, so that they belch out of their black mouths the most dreadful curses. What can be said of such tongues, but that they are “set on fire by hell”?#footnote[Jas 3:6.] As the other failings for which masters are reproved are to be applied to mistresses, so this one especially; for mistresses commonly offend most in shrewishness of speech.

3\. In #emph[actions,] when masters are too frequent and too furious #footnote[#emph[Quamuis dominus seruo praestet, tamen se aequalem iudicet quatenus homo existit. Constit. Apost. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 12.] strikers, striking their servants on every occasion, not caring how they strike. In these and other such signs of too much severity and plain arrogance, masters forget that they are men. Though for outward order a master is more excellent than a servant, yet as a man he ought to judge himself his equal. This extreme can be no good means of maintaining authority; it is plainly an abuse of it. Masters ought so #footnote[#emph[Serui colant potius te quàm timeant. Senec. epist.] 47.] to conduct themselves that their servants may rather reverence than dread them.

=== §. 7. #emph[Of masters’ commanding power limited to lawful things.] #footnote[Extent of a master’s power.]

So that a master may know better how to maintain his authority, I will set out distinctly the #emph[extent] of his power, and that in these four particulars: 1. In #emph[commanding.] 2. In #emph[correcting.] 3. In #emph[ordering the marriage of their servants.] 4. In #emph[having the disposal of their persons.]

Within the bounds of these two virtues, #emph[Justice] and #emph[Equity] (to which all the duties that masters owe their servants will #footnote[§. 20, 21, #emph[etc.]] afterward be referred), the commanding power of masters must be confined.

+ #emph[Justice] requires two things:
  + A #emph[restraint] on masters’ commandments.
  + An #emph[exercise] of them.

The restraint is to God’s law: that a master command nothing against it,#footnote[Nothing unlawful is to be commanded by masters. 1 Sam 26:9.] but only what agrees with it. Abishai would gladly have had David’s warrant to kill Saul; but David was so far from commanding him to do it that he kept him from it.

Masters are only subordinate ministers under God; they must therefore command nothing against his law. As a judge, a high sheriff and all other officers under the king must make the king’s law the ground of everything they require of the king’s subjects, so masters must make God’s law the ground of everything they require of their servants, who are also the servants of Christ. Besides, what is the point of commanding what a servant may and must refuse to do? But in no #footnote[Treatise 7, §. 38.] unlawful thing may he obey.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of masters presuming beyond their authority.]

Contrary to this is the arrogance and presumption of many masters who make their own will the rule of their servants’ obedience. This must be a leaden rule, which may be bent this way, that way and every way, because of the flexible and changeable whims of man. That is why many most vile and horrible things are commanded: because they suit the whim of the one who commands. Absalom ordered his servants to commit a most detestable murder on his own brother, and note how he presses it: “Have I not commanded you#super[+]?” #footnote[2 Sam 13:28.]

More presumptuous is the one who takes it on himself to be the #footnote[The pope’s presumption.] great commander of all Christians, the pope of Rome, and other masters and governors of Jesuits, friars, monks and other such orders, who command those under their authority to commit treasons, raise rebellions, kill kings and do other such abominable villainies.

And to justify the pope, from whom all inferior governors receive their power and by whose will they must be ruled, papists hold these blasphemous positions about his power: “He can do all things by right, as God. He is as God, having #footnote[#emph[Extravag. de major. & obed. Alban. in lib. de potestat. Pap. & Eccles. Gratian. in Decret. dist.] 40.] on earth fullness of power. If he should draw innumerable souls of men with him into hell, yet no one may say to him, ‘What are you doing?’” Does not the apostle’s #footnote[2 Thess 2:4. #emph[In Ecclesia cath. semper creditum est Rom. Pontificem posse sua authoritate leges ferre, quae in conscientia obligent. Bellar. de Rom. Pontif. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 15.] description of “the man of sin, the son of destruction” fit him of whom these things are said? Is he not, in the papists’ account, “as God … proclaiming himself to be God”? This is further confirmed by the power which they give him of making laws to bind the conscience, and of coining new articles of faith.

Not only popish masters, but profane ones too, go too far in this presumptuous use of their power beyond their power: such as those who order their servants to kill, to steal, to swear, to perjure themselves, to lie, to give false measures and weights, to go to Mass, to profane the Sabbath, and to commit other such sins. In all these, and in all other things like them that are against God’s law, masters have no power to command. They go beyond their commission, and will answer for it accordingly.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of masters commanding servants to do their duty.]

2\. The exercise of a master’s commanding power consists in the things which God has enjoined as binding duties. These a master must command his servants to do by virtue of his authority: such as to worship God, to sanctify his Sabbath, to be diligent and conscientious in his calling, with other such duties, which God sums up in these words: “the way of the #smallcaps[Lord] … what is right and just.” And God says that he knew that Abraham would “command … his household” to do them. #footnote[Gen 18:19.]

The charge given to masters in the fourth commandment concerning servants (“neither you … nor your manservant or maidservant”) #footnote[Exod 20:10.] proves as much. Josiah#footnote[2 Chr 34:32.] is commended for making his people stand by the covenant of God. On this ground, if a master has servants who are papists, separatists or profane persons, he ought to command them, and make them go to the holy ordinances of God.

It is one of the chief ends for which God has given power and authority to some over others, that by their authority they may make those who are under them keep the commandments of God, just as inferior officers have authority given them to see that the king’s laws are kept.

=== §. 10. #emph[Of the sin of masters in allowing servants to neglect their duty.]

It is contrary to the commanding power which God has given to masters to allow their servants to omit and neglect the binding duties which God has commanded them: as when a servant is so popish or profane that he refuses to go to the word, or the sacrament, or to perform any duty to which he is bound, and his master lets him alone. Though masters do those duties themselves, and though they do not hinder their servants and keep them from doing them, yet if they do not make their servants do them too, they make themselves accessories to their servants’ sin.

#emph[Objection.] Every man’s conscience is free and cannot be forced; therefore masters must not compel servants to things that are against their conscience.

#emph[Answer.] Though the conscience is free to a man, and out of another’s power, yet men’s outward actions are not free; and though neither faith, piety, righteousness nor any grace can be forced into men, yet they may be forced to use the means which God has sanctified for breeding and increasing them. Though they cannot be forced to have a religious and righteous heart, yet they may be forced to do religious and righteous duties. Or if a master cannot force these, yet he may and ought to do his utmost; and by this means, though he cannot free his servant from death, yet he will free his own soul from the guilt of his servant’s death.

=== §. 11. #emph[Of a master’s wisdom in ordering things neither commanded nor forbidden.]

Equity has respect to the things which are in a master’s power to command or not to command. Such are all secular actions that concern himself, about the particulars of which God has given no direct charge that they must be absolutely performed: as to go on such an errand, to deliver such a message, to carry out such a piece of business. Concerning these things I may say of a master’s power what the apostle says of a parent’s power#footnote[1 Cor 7:36, 37.] in another case: if he sees fit for it to be done, and commands his servant to do it, “he does well, he does not sin”; if he does not see fit for it to be done, and does not command it, “he also does well, he does not sin.” The centurion #footnote[Matt 8:9.] speaks of this kind of thing where he says that he tells one, “Go,” another, “Come,” and a third, “Do this.”

The mark at which masters must aim in commanding or forbidding these things must be expediency; for “not all things #footnote[1 Cor 10:23.] that are lawful are expedient.” Expediency depends much on circumstances, and on the consequences which may follow from doing anything; and in observing these the wisdom of the one who has power to have a thing done or not done especially appears. When David did not allow Hushai,#footnote[2 Sam 15:33, 34.] his good friend and wise counselor, to go with him when he fled from Absalom, but told him to return to the city and stay there, he had an eye to the good consequence that might follow from it. And when Joab commanded the Cushite rather than Ahimaaz to #footnote[2 Sam 18:20, 21.] carry the news of Absalom’s death, he had an eye to the fitness of the persons, and to the kind of message.

To apply this to our purpose: #emph[Equity] requires that masters (in commanding things which God neither commands nor forbids, and which they have power to command or not to command) have an eye to their servants’ ability, sex, age, disposition, conscience and other such circumstances, so that the thing which they command is in some measure suited to them: not above their ability, not unbecoming their sex, not unfitting their age, not thwarting their disposition, not against their conscience.

=== §. 12. #emph[Of masters’ offense against expediency.]

It is contrary to equity for a master to regard only himself and his own mind, even in things that are lawful. Paul was not #footnote[1 Cor 10:33.] of this mind when he said that he did not seek his own profit; no, nor did he seek his own will. Expediency and inexpediency were great motives to him to refrain from things which were otherwise lawful. Yet many pay little regard to this, as these instances show:

1\. Many command things to the very utmost of their servants’ strength, if not beyond it (as Pharaoh did),#footnote[Exod 5:7.] or else dangerous things, which may bring great injury on them. David #footnote[2 Sam 23:17.] was touched in heart for moving his servants, by a mere wish, to fetch him water at the risk of their lives.

2\. Others, against propriety, put men to do maids’ work, and maids to do men’s work.

3\. Others, out of mere partiality, keep old and good servants down, and promote much younger ones over them.

4\. Others will forcibly make servants do things against their natural disposition, such as handling, and even eating, things they cannot bear to touch or see.

5\. Others in doubtful matters will urge and press them against their conscience.

Although in these and other such things that are not unlawful servants ought to do what they can to satisfy their master’s command, if he is peremptory in urging them, yet a master ought not to be too peremptory in pressing his authority and power.
